Description:
EctoCellect is a prospective company that has developed a high quality resource of human cell surface receptor proteins to improve the development of new biological therapeutics. Our core offering is a curated genome-scale resource consisting of the entire ectodomains of human receptor proteins arrayed for systematic screening. 

This technology can be used for:

- Drug target identification, particularly identifying binding partners for new cancer checkpoint inhibitors or targetting inhibitory receptors;

- Safety testing new medicines such as therapeutic antibodies and chimeric antigen receptors (CARs) for any potential off-target toxicity effects;

- Understanding the mechanism of action of proposed new drug targets (e.g. targets of therapeutic antibodies);

- Target deconvolution to identify binding specificities of functional antibodies of unknown binding specificity; 

- Discovering the binding profiles of auto-antisera to identify targets of antibody-based autoimmunity; 

- Host-pathogen interactions including virus receptors and invasion receptors used by bacteria, parasites, and fungi.

This trusted technology is backed by over a decade of publications showing it can be used at scale to identify new human leukocyte receptor-ligand interactions (Shilts et al. “A physical wiring diagram for the human immune system” Nature 608, p397–404, 2022) and blood stage malaria vaccine targets (Crosnier et al. “Basigin is a receptor essential for erythrocyte invasion by Plasmodium falciparum” Nature 480, p534–537, 2011).

In February 1996 “International Strategy Meeting on Human Genome Sequencing” was held in the islands of Bermuda as it is a neutral meeting location. The goal during this gathering was to negotiate the fate of the gene sequences being deduced by The Human Genome Project (HGP). To the benefit of the global scientific community, they collectively held strong to the conviction that medical advancement should overrule financial gain. The Bermuda Principles agreement cemented the legacy of these values and all gene sequences were issued freely to the worldwide community. 

The Bermuda Principles Foundation was founded to continue the legacy of the Bermuda Principles on current medical research and its impact on the world.  

The main aims of the foundation are: 

(i) to advance the education of the general public, and especially the youth, on careers related to science; 

(ii) to provide a productive platform for carrying out scientific research; 

(iii) to promote and provide local opportunities for scientific engagement on topics that are relevant and of common interest to improve the health of the general public and the environment; 

(iv) to provide ongoing support and develop partnerships, whether local or international, which encourage the development of scientists in Bermuda, putting Bermudians on the world map

(v) to raise awareness of Bermuda’s scientific cultural heritage and historic ties to the advancement of biological and scientific research.

Ithaka Life Sciences (Ithaka) provides expert advice, guidance, and hands-on support for organisations in the life sciences sector. The team of industry experts have worked closely with companies ranging from SMEs to multinationals, universities and research organisations, investors, government agencies, the NHS and other public sector bodies to support them in the commercialisation of life science technologies.

Originally founded by Dr Paul Rodgers in 2000, Ithaka has delivered approximately 200 assignments for 100 clients, founded eight university spin-out companies, and helped early stage companies to raise in excess of £30m of investment finance.

With its unique network of contacts, breadth and depth of experience across the life science sector, and a proven track record in achieving client satisfaction, there are many reasons for choosing to work with Ithaka.

Our expert team specialises in: 
•	Drug discovery and development; 
•	Bio-therapeutics; 
•	Biomarkers and personalised medicine; 
•	Life science and biomedical research tools; 
•	Medical devices, diagnostics and sensors.

We work closely with SMEs, multinationals, universities, research organisations, investors, government agencies, and public sector bodies.

Our Life Science services include:  
•	Technical, commercial and market evaluation of novel technologies; 
•	Commercial strategy, business creation and business planning; 
•	Sourcing investment finance; 
•	Hands-on company management; 
•	Coaching and mentoring support; 
•	Business development; 
•	Strategic marketing and licensing; 
•	Intellectual property review.

